==English==
{{wp|malice (law)}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|malice}}, borrowed from {{der|en|fro|malice}}, from {{der|en|la|malitia|t=badness, bad quality, ill-will, spite}}, from {{m|la|malus|t=bad}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|măl'ĭs}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈmælɪs/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-malice.ogg|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|s}}

# Intention to harm or deprive in an illegal or immoral way. Desire to take pleasure in another's misfortune.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1981|author=w:Philip K. Dick|title=Valis|isbn=0-553-20594-3|page=67
|passage={{...}} not only was there no gratitude (which he could psychologically handle) but downright '''malice''' showed itself instead.}}
# {{lbl|en|law}} An intention to do [[injury]] to another [[party]], which in many [[jurisdiction]]s is a distinguishing factor between the [[crime]]s of [[murder]] and [[manslaughter]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Sam Levine; Kira Lerner|title=Fox and Dominion settle for US$787.5m in defamation lawsuit over election lies|work=The Guardian|date=2023-04-18|url=https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2023/apr/18/fox-dominion-settle-us-defamation-lawsuit|issn=0261-3077|passage=The question that would have been before the jury was whether Fox committed “actual '''malice'''” in airing the claims. That required Dominion to show whether key decision makers were aware the claims were false or acted with reckless disregard for the truth.}}

